    "b-sks-rc-evidence-intro": {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "props": {},
      "text": [
        {
          "insert": "EVIDENCE — An "
        },
        {
          "insert": "evidence",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " row is one citation hanging off a claim: a typed "
        },
        {
          "insert": "locator",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " into a knowledge source and a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "why",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " saying how the pointed-at record supports the owning fact. Each row belongs to exactly one fact through "
        },
        {
          "insert": "fact_id",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": "; two facts leaning on the same Discord message hold two evidence rows, and the source row is the shared thing. Nothing is snapshotted — no content is stored on the row; display resolves the "
        },
        {
          "insert": "locator",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " into the source at read time. Only "
        },
        {
          "insert": "code",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " rows carry a "
        },
        {
          "insert": "digest",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", pinning the span text against the one source that drifts. "
        },
        {
          "insert": "why",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": " is per-pointer; the claim-level argument that synthesizes the citations is the fact's "
        },
        {
          "insert": "rationale",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ". The librarian writes evidence rows in the same write as their fact: an overwrite of the fact replaces its rows wholesale, a clear deletes them with it, and a row is immutable while it lives. Reverse lookup — what stands on this source record — is an indexed query on ("
        },
        {
          "insert": "kind",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ", "
        },
        {
          "insert": "locator",
          "attributes": {
            "code": true
          }
        },
        {
          "insert": ")."
        }
      ],
      "id": "b-sks-rc-evidence-intro",
      "children": []
    },
